E('enlightenment|age of enlightenment|age of reason', 'The Enlightenment was an intellectual movement that championed reason, science and individual rights.', 'It flourished in Europe in the 17th and 18th centuries.',
  'Thinkers questioned the authority of kings and churches.', 'Key figures include John Locke, Voltaire, Rousseau, Montesquieu, Kant and Adam Smith.', 'They promoted ideas like freedom of speech, religious tolerance and separation of powers.', 'Diderot’s Encyclopédie tried to gather all human knowledge.',
  'It inspired the American and French revolutions and modern democracy.')
E('john locke', 'John Locke was an English philosopher, often called the father of liberalism.', 'He lived from 1632 to 1704.',
  'He argued people have natural rights to life, liberty and property.', 'He said governments get their authority from the consent of the governed.', 'If a government breaks that trust, people may replace it.', 'He described the mind at birth as a “blank slate”.',
  'His ideas strongly influenced the American Declaration of Independence.')
E('voltaire', 'Voltaire was a French writer and philosopher of the Enlightenment.', 'He lived from 1694 to 1778; his real name was François-Marie Arouet.',
  'He championed freedom of speech, religious tolerance and separation of church and state.', 'He wrote the satirical novel Candide.', 'He was imprisoned in the Bastille and exiled for his writing.',
  'A famous summary of his views, “I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it”, was actually written by a biographer.')
E('rousseau|jean-jacques rousseau', 'Jean-Jacques Rousseau was a Geneva-born philosopher of the Enlightenment.', 'He lived from 1712 to 1778.',
  'His book The Social Contract begins: “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.”', 'He argued legitimate power comes from the “general will” of the people.', 'He believed people are naturally good but corrupted by society.',
  'He inspired the French Revolution and later Romanticism.')
E('adam smith|wealth of nations', 'Adam Smith was a Scottish economist and philosopher, often called the father of modern economics.', 'He lived from 1723 to 1790.',
  'His book The Wealth of Nations was published in 1776.', 'He argued that free markets guided by an “invisible hand” can benefit society.', 'He explained how dividing labour into tasks boosts productivity, using a pin factory example.', 'He also wrote about morality and sympathy in The Theory of Moral Sentiments.',
  'His ideas underpin modern capitalism and economics.')
E('american revolution|american revolutionary war|american war of independence', 'The American Revolution was the struggle by thirteen British colonies to win independence.', 'The war lasted from 1775 to 1783.',
  'Colonists protested taxes passed without their representation in Parliament.', 'The Boston Tea Party in 1773 was a famous protest.', 'Fighting began at Lexington and Concord in April 1775.', 'The Declaration of Independence was adopted on 4 July 1776.', 'France’s help was crucial, and the British surrendered at Yorktown in 1781.', 'The Treaty of Paris recognised American independence in 1783.',
  'It created the United States and inspired revolutions elsewhere.')
E('declaration of independence|july 4 1776|fourth of july|independence day', 'The Declaration of Independence announced that the thirteen American colonies were free from Britain.', 'It was adopted on 4 July 1776.',
  'It was mainly written by Thomas Jefferson.', 'It says “all men are created equal” with rights to “Life, Liberty and the pursuit of Happiness”.', 'It listed complaints against King George III.', 'John Hancock’s large signature made “John Hancock” slang for a signature.',
  'Its words became a reference point for later movements for freedom and equality.')
E('george washington|washington', 'George Washington was the commander of the American army in the Revolution and the first President of the United States.', 'He lived from 1732 to 1799 and was president from 1789 to 1797.',
  'He led the Continental Army against the British.', 'He crossed the icy Delaware River on Christmas night, 1776, to win at Trenton.', 'He presided over the convention that wrote the Constitution.', 'He voluntarily stepped down after two terms, setting a precedent.', 'The story of him chopping down a cherry tree is a myth.',
  'He is called the “Father of His Country”, and the US capital is named after him.')
E('thomas jefferson|jefferson', 'Thomas Jefferson was a Founding Father, the main author of the Declaration of Independence and the third US President.', 'He lived from 1743 to 1826 and was president from 1801 to 1809.',
  'He arranged the Louisiana Purchase in 1803, doubling the size of the country.', 'He sent Lewis and Clark to explore the West.', 'He founded the University of Virginia.', 'He enslaved more than 600 people during his life, a stark contradiction with his words on liberty.', 'He died on 4 July 1826, the same day as John Adams.',
  'His ideals of liberty and his contradictions both shape American history.')
E('benjamin franklin|franklin', 'Benjamin Franklin was an American statesman, scientist, inventor and writer.', 'He lived from 1706 to 1790.',
  'He helped draft the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution.', 'His famous kite experiment explored electricity.', 'He invented the lightning rod, bifocal glasses and the Franklin stove.', 'He secured vital French support during the Revolution.', 'He published Poor Richard’s Almanack.',
  'He is pictured on the US hundred-dollar bill.')
E('us constitution|united states constitution|constitution|bill of rights', 'The United States Constitution is the supreme law of the US.', 'It was written in 1787 and took effect in 1789.',
  'It set up three branches of government: legislative, executive and judicial.', 'It created a system of checks and balances.', 'The first ten amendments, the Bill of Rights, were added in 1791.', 'They protect freedoms like speech, religion and the press.', 'It has been amended 27 times.',
  'It is the oldest written national constitution still in use.')
E('french revolution|storming of the bastille|bastille', 'The French Revolution overthrew the monarchy and transformed France.', 'It began in 1789 and lasted until about 1799.',
  'France was bankrupt, food was expensive, and ordinary people paid most of the taxes.', 'Crowds stormed the Bastille prison in Paris on 14 July 1789, now France’s national day.', 'The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen was issued in 1789.', 'King Louis XVI and Queen Marie Antoinette were executed in 1793.', 'During the Reign of Terror, thousands were guillotined.', 'Napoleon seized power in 1799.',
  'Its slogan “Liberty, Equality, Fraternity” still inspires movements around the world.')
E('marie antoinette', 'Marie Antoinette was the last queen of France before the Revolution.', 'She lived from 1755 to 1793.',
  'She was born an Austrian archduchess and married the future Louis XVI.', 'She became a symbol of royal extravagance.', 'She almost certainly never said “Let them eat cake”.', 'She was executed by guillotine in October 1793.',
  'She remains one of history’s most famous and misunderstood royals.')
E('robespierre|reign of terror|the terror', 'The Reign of Terror was a period of mass executions during the French Revolution, led by Maximilien Robespierre.', 'It lasted from 1793 to 1794.',
  'The revolutionary government executed suspected enemies.', 'Around 17,000 people were officially executed, many by guillotine.', 'Robespierre himself was guillotined in July 1794.',
  'It became a warning about revolutions turning violent.')
E('napoleon|napoleon bonaparte|bonaparte', 'Napoleon Bonaparte was a French military leader who became Emperor of France.', 'He lived from 1769 to 1821 and crowned himself emperor in 1804.',
  'He was born in Corsica and rose quickly during the French Revolution.', 'He conquered much of Europe in the Napoleonic Wars.', 'His Napoleonic Code reformed law and still influences legal systems worldwide.', 'His invasion of Russia in 1812 was a disaster.', 'He was exiled to Elba, escaped, and was finally defeated at Waterloo in 1815.', 'He died in exile on Saint Helena.', 'He wasn’t especially short; he was about average height for his time.',
  'He is still one of the most studied commanders and leaders in history.')
E('waterloo|battle of waterloo', 'The Battle of Waterloo was Napoleon’s final defeat.', 'It was fought on 18 June 1815, in what is now Belgium.',
  'The Duke of Wellington commanded a British-led allied army.', 'Prussian troops under Blücher arrived to help decisively.', 'Napoleon abdicated days later and was exiled.',
  'It ended the Napoleonic Wars, and “meeting your Waterloo” now means a final defeat.')
E('industrial revolution', 'The Industrial Revolution was the shift from hand production to machines and factories.', 'It began in Britain around 1760 and spread to Europe and America in the 19th century.',
  'Coal and steam power drove new machines.', 'The textile industry was transformed by inventions like the spinning jenny.', 'James Watt’s improved steam engine was key.', 'Railways and canals moved goods quickly.', 'Cities grew rapidly, often with terrible living conditions.', 'Child labour was common in factories and mines.',
  'It created the modern economy and changed how most people lived and worked.')
E('james watt|steam engine', 'James Watt was a Scottish engineer who greatly improved the steam engine.', 'He lived from 1736 to 1819.',
  'His separate condenser, patented in 1769, made steam engines far more efficient.', 'His engines powered factories, mines and mills.', 'The unit of power, the watt, is named after him.',
  'His work powered the Industrial Revolution.')
E('railways|first railway|stephenson|rocket locomotive', 'Railways transformed travel and trade in the 19th century.', 'The first public steam railway, the Stockton and Darlington, opened in England in 1825.',
  'George Stephenson and his son Robert built the famous locomotive Rocket in 1829.', 'The Liverpool and Manchester Railway opened in 1830.', 'Railways spread quickly across Europe and North America.', 'The US transcontinental railroad was completed in 1869.',
  'Railways created standard time zones and connected whole nations.')
E('simon bolivar|bolivar|bolívar', 'Simón Bolívar was a Venezuelan leader who freed much of South America from Spanish rule.', 'He lived from 1783 to 1830.',
  'He is called El Libertador, “the Liberator”.', 'He led independence wars for Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia.', 'Bolivia is named after him.', 'He dreamed of a united South America, Gran Colombia, which broke apart.',
  'He is a national hero across Latin America.')
E('haitian revolution|toussaint louverture', 'The Haitian Revolution was a successful uprising of enslaved people in the French colony of Saint-Domingue.', 'It lasted from 1791 to 1804.',
  'It was led at first by Toussaint Louverture, a formerly enslaved man.', 'It defeated French, Spanish and British forces.', 'Haiti declared independence in 1804.',
  'It created the first nation founded by formerly enslaved people and the first Black republic.')
E('queen victoria|victoria|victorian era|victorian', 'Queen Victoria was Queen of the United Kingdom during a period of vast British power.', 'She lived from 1819 to 1901 and reigned from 1837.',
  'She reigned for 63 years, a record later broken by Elizabeth II.', 'She married Prince Albert, and they had nine children.', 'After Albert died in 1861 she wore black for the rest of her life.', 'She became Empress of India in 1876.', 'Her children married into many royal families, earning her the nickname “grandmother of Europe”.',
  'The Victorian era is linked with industry, empire and strict social values.')
E('british empire', 'The British Empire was the largest empire in history.', 'It peaked in the early 20th century, covering about a quarter of the world’s land.',
  'It was said “the sun never set” on it.', 'It included India, Canada, Australia and large parts of Africa.', 'Trade, industry and naval power drove its growth.', 'It brought exploitation, violence and famine to many places it ruled.', 'Most colonies gained independence after the Second World War.',
  'The Commonwealth of Nations and the global spread of English are part of its legacy.')
E('american civil war|civil war', 'The American Civil War was fought between the northern states, the Union, and southern states that seceded, the Confederacy.', 'It lasted from 1861 to 1865.',
  'The central cause was slavery and whether it would expand.', 'Abraham Lincoln’s election led eleven southern states to secede.', 'The Battle of Gettysburg in 1863 was a turning point.', 'About 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers died, more than in any other American war.', 'The Confederacy surrendered at Appomattox in April 1865.', 'Slavery was abolished by the 13th Amendment in 1865.',
  'It preserved the Union and ended slavery, but the struggle for equality continued.')
E('abraham lincoln|lincoln|gettysburg address', 'Abraham Lincoln was the 16th President of the United States, who led the country through the Civil War.', 'He lived from 1809 to 1865 and was president from 1861.',
  'He grew up poor and largely taught himself.', 'He issued the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863.', 'His Gettysburg Address called for “government of the people, by the people, for the people”.', 'He pushed for the 13th Amendment to abolish slavery.', 'He was assassinated by John Wilkes Booth at Ford’s Theatre in April 1865.',
  'He is often ranked as one of the greatest American presidents.')
E('harriet tubman', 'Harriet Tubman was an American abolitionist who escaped slavery and helped others escape.', 'She lived from about 1822 to 1913.',
  'She escaped from Maryland in 1849.', 'She returned about thirteen times to lead around seventy people to freedom on the Underground Railroad.', 'She served as a scout and spy for the Union army.', 'She later campaigned for women’s right to vote.',
  'She is an enduring symbol of courage and freedom.')
E('frederick douglass', 'Frederick Douglass was an American abolitionist, writer and speaker who escaped slavery.', 'He lived from about 1818 to 1895.',
  'He escaped slavery in 1838.', 'His autobiography, published in 1845, exposed the horrors of slavery.', 'He became one of the most famous speakers of his age.', 'He advised Abraham Lincoln.',
  'He is remembered as one of the great voices for freedom and equality.')
E('meiji restoration|meiji', 'The Meiji Restoration returned power to Japan’s emperor and launched rapid modernisation.', 'It began in 1868.',
  'It ended the rule of the Tokugawa shoguns.', 'Japan adopted Western technology, industry and military methods.', 'The samurai class was abolished.', 'Within decades, Japan became a major world power.',
  'It is one of history’s fastest transformations of a nation.')
E('unification of italy|garibaldi|risorgimento', 'The unification of Italy, the Risorgimento, joined separate states into one kingdom.', 'It took place mainly between 1848 and 1871.',
  'Giuseppe Garibaldi led his “Redshirts” to conquer Sicily and Naples in 1860.', 'Count Cavour masterminded the diplomacy.', 'Victor Emmanuel II became the first king of Italy in 1861.', 'Rome became the capital in 1871.',
  'It created the modern Italian state.')
E('unification of germany|bismarck|otto von bismarck', 'Otto von Bismarck was the Prussian statesman who unified Germany.', 'He lived from 1815 to 1898; Germany was unified in 1871.',
  'He used a series of short wars against Denmark, Austria and France.', 'Wilhelm I was proclaimed German Emperor at Versailles in 1871.', 'Bismarck became Germany’s first chancellor.', 'He introduced early forms of social insurance and pensions.',
  'He was called the “Iron Chancellor”, and his diplomacy shaped Europe for decades.')
